<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dharmashastra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dharmashastra is the body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> treatises on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, the religious and legal duties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, of which the Laws of Manu is the best known. They cover the duties of kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, family law, inheritance and penance. Adapted versions shaped law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, including the Thai and Burmese legal codes, and British courts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> applied them as Hindu law.</p></div>
